 really able to have nice  consistent high level expansion  of these cells  that retain their function  um so anyway this has been a great uh  collaboration with thermo and we've been  able to  benefit from that by uh by adopting some  of these media  and hopefully being able to provide some  good data back to them as well and as  they  move their product along  so i'll end with just a couple of slides  about where do we go next um and how do  we now take this platform and move it  into something that is even more  applicable to uh to treating large  number patients or specific disease  types  so the first is  to really change how we think about  donors um i mentioned at the beginning  that that  um there's a lot of donor variability  and that's one of those things you want  to control for  but the field has actually moved along  in several steps  the first nk cell studies were all done  with autologous enquiry cells because  they're the safest obviously if you get  your own cells back there's they're  probably not going to hurt you  but that situation um  was really fraught with problems of of  failing  because of the cells themselves so  um we tried to do  initially uh like late 90s early 2000s  some autologous cells  studies were done in lymphoma and nobody  ever saw any responses well we thought  that was probably because  the nk cells themselves were a little  bit dysfunctional  and so we did some experiments to see if  expansion would improve the function of  those cells and it does they completely  restore their function  but the patients themselves are really  um  so beat up by the chemotherapy that some  patients we just couldn't get enough  cells to start from and so we couldn't  get enough cells for adequate doses  so that's the problem with the first box  is that you have failed expansions you  just can't treat everybody  the second  move then is to go to family donors and  identify um allogeneic donors for each  patient which is we usually refer to as  a haploidentical donors and in that  setting sometimes patients don't have a  donor or they don't have an ideal donor  they have two or three people who could  donate some cells a mom and a dad and a  brother  for most of our kids or uh you know a  son or a daughter  um but not everybody has a donor so then  you have patients who just can't be  treated  so the real question for us is could we  identify what we thought were  the ideal characteristics of a donor and  collect large numbers of cells from  those  individuals and create banks of cells  that could be used as an off-the-shelf  approach  for what we would call like a universal  donor and we called a universal donor  similar to the the idea of um blood  banking where if you are  uh o  o negative if you're a negative you can  give blood to anybody because there's  nobody who will reject your blood  and the same idea here if we can get if  we can identify the right universal  donor frank k cells that one donor could  be given to anybody  so  how do we do that um so we really poured  through the literature around what makes  a good donor and we settled on some  things like having high qb content  that those have been  associated with better outcomes in most  transplant studies  we want all the the inhibitory cures to  be licensed that's what gives nk cells a  more functional  phenotype we want  patients donors to have had prior cmv  because that's what induces a particular  protein called nkg2c and make some  adaptive nk cells and we want to make  sure that that those  donors have adequate numbers of nk cells  that they proliferate okay so we've been  screening them so we've been working for  about a year and a half with a group  called be the match biotherapies which  is the  um sort of non-transplant arm of the  nmdp national marrow donor program and  so nmdp if you go to a bone marrow drive  they're the ones who swab your cheek and  put you in a database and so they've got  a database of hundreds of thousands of  individuals who already have had a lot  of the testing that we would need  and so we've been working in about a  year and a half to  do all of this screening and collection  and collect batches of  products  from these donors and then expand them  for therapy and  then from each individual you can  actually collect enough material to  treat  dozens if not hundreds of patients  so we've really just started on that  we've treated the first six patients now  with the universal donor nk cells  we have a couple of patients that we've  been able to  to follow those cells by hla haplotype  differences  and see that the nk cells after we  infuse them go to really really high  numbers are sustained for a couple of  weeks and then drop back down again  afterwards so we're really encouraged by  this as an approach this will greatly  streamline um the clinical trials  process  lots of patients can't wait for nk cells  to be manufactured for them they need  the cells now  and it also has  significantly reduced our cost of goods  probably by  eighty percent uh in terms of of the  cost per dose uh of nk cells that we  were delivering  so another thing that we need to try to  overcome for solid tumors is  the presence of tgf-beta and so we  decided to try  just as a  a simple experiment in the lab if we add  tgf-beta to our  culture method  maybe we could suppress the cells that  are sensitive and the ones that are  resistant would grow out and we could  generate these tgf-beta resistant nk  cells  well turns out the tj beta does nothing  to cell proliferation  it only inhibits cytokine production and  a little bit of cytotoxicity but it  doesn't impact the proliferation of the  cells so it completely failed from that  perspective of what we thought was going  to happen  but what did happen is when the tgf-beta  was present for  the whole time of our culture then when  you took them out of that environment  they suddenly now are tgf-beta resistant  they make even more cytokine than they  did before  and they've completely shut off  this protein called samad 3 which is an  important part of tgf-beta signaling so  that's why they're now tjp beta  resistant they can't signal from the  cytokine  but they've been sort of revved up in  terms of their ability to make uh  further other functional effects  and surprisingly even when we're looking  at things like long-term cytotoxicity  um the green and the red in let's just  look at the bottom left-hand graph this  is kinetic killing  over a three-day period of an aml cell  line called kasumi and the green is the  wild type at low et ratios and the red  is the wild type at high e t ratios  the pink is the low e t ratio with tg  beta and printing and the blue is the  high e t ratio with tg beta imprinting  so the tgf beta cells are not just  um  able to make more cytokine they're not  just able to overcome tj beta but they  actually kill  long term even better  so we're working on that  to get it to the clinic in a dog model  the last thing i'll show you is is we've  adapted this approach to growing cells  from  from blood bank dogs  osu across the street  has a a doggy blood bank where they  collect blood all the time for their  their canine patients and we're doing  the same thing as we would for humans  take their buffy coat get rid of the t  cells expand them uh on our feeder cell  cryopreserve them and so we now have  about a half a dozen dogs  with cancer that we've treated with this  universal donor approach of tgf beta  resistance cells  so more to come  more for us to kind of optimize and we  now have a nice  wonderful model to use for how to better  deliver these cells  so with that i'll just say thank you to  an awful lot of people that i work with  um who  help in the lab um former lab members  who have developed a lot of this  um collaboratives that we've had all  across the world lucia sila is a  collaborator in brazil she's established  a whole program down there of delivering  nk cells  that we've published together with  university of montreal where we've  worked with  some gene modification  lots of foundations that have really  supported our work and people in the gmp  facility and regulatory ip and  operations that have helped us actually  get our clinical trials going  and with that  this is our team actually this is a  couple years ago we have a lot of new  members and some of these have moved on  but  a large team that all is necessary to  make this happen  and i will  move on to some questions  and thank you so much dr lee for your  time today and we will now start the  live q and a portion of this webinar if  you have any questions you want to ask  please do so now just click on that ask  a question box located on the far left  of your screen and we'll answer as many  questions as we have time for  so let's take a look we already have  some great questions coming in dr lee  our first question  in your opinion can aim the medium plus  icsr supplement be used in expanding  pluripotent stem cell-derived nk cells  in feeder-free system  i think it's likely  um  so we we've actually used the feeder  the feeder cell system um to expand nk  cells that come from  ipscs uh and that approach is actually  what um what fade is using now in their  production so they use a feeder cell  approach and  once you get to the nk cell stage the  expansion part seems to be the same  whether the nk cells come from  ipscs or cord blood or hematopoietic  stem cells like um  [Music]  i'm liking on the company but uh encored  um  or from peripheral blood uh it doesn't  really matter the the proliferation part  is the same so i would expect that the  serum free media would work the same for  theirs the key is i think for them or  other companies that are ipsc related  is the  pre-enquestion piece what are you  growing the ipscs in and what what are  you doing your differentiation of ipscs  into nk cells from the nk cells on they  all seem to behave the same  tha